# Account deposits

`Account.deposits` is a list of contributions into that account: monthly,
yearly, or one-time, with a required `startYear` and an optional `endYear`. The
amount is **flat** for the life of the deposit: there is deliberately no
escalation rate (an earlier version had one and it was removed, so don't
reintroduce it without being asked).

## The pre-plan rule (the thing to get right)

The projection starts at the earliest retirement year, and `account.balance` is
January 1 of that year. Most deposits are contributions made while still
working, so they are dated **before** the plan starts: those years are never
projected, so they cannot touch cash flow, taxes, or surplus.

- Before the start year: compounded into the opening balance by
  `preStartDepositValue` (DROP adds principal only: it does not grow until
  the year after the owner retires), and (for after-tax accounts) added to
  the opening cost basis by `preStartDepositPrincipal`.
- From the start year on: credited inside the projection loop, and subtracted
  from `surplus` like an expense.

Inside the loop, deposits land **after** growth and withdrawals (step 3b in
`project.ts`, right after conversions), so a deposit starts compounding the
following year. Extending that backwards is what fixes the pre-start
compounding exponent: a deposit in year `Y` lands on January 1 of `Y + 1`, so it
grows for `start - Y - 1` years. Keep those two in sync or the boundary year
double-counts (or loses) a year of growth.

## Tax treatment follows the account kind

| Account kind | Deposit effect |
| ---- | ---- |
| `retirementTaxable` | Pre-tax: subtracted from federal and state ordinary gross (floored at 0) before deductions, and reported as `row.preTaxDeposits` |
| `rothTaxFree` | Post-tax: balance only |
| after-tax (`investment`, `annuity`, `cd`, `savings`) | Adds cost basis, both the running `basisRemaining` (so withdrawals realize less gain) and `afterTaxCostBasis` in `runScenario.ts` (so the estate isn't taxed on returned basis) |

## Three places mirror the same math

`deposits.ts` is the single source. If you change deposit timing, update all
three callers together:

1. `project.ts`: opening balance, opening basis, per-year credit.
2. `convertible.ts`: the conversion cap estimate re-runs growth and withdrawals,
   so it needs the deposits too or the cap is understated.
3. `runScenario.ts`: `afterTaxCostBasis(household, throughYear)` is year-aware
   because basis grows as deposits are paid in.

## Row fields and UI

`ProjectionRow` gained `monthlyDeposits`, `depositMonthly` (per deposit id), and
`preTaxDeposits`; `surplus` is now
`netMonthlyIncome - monthlyExpenses - monthlyDeposits`. Plans without deposits
produce identical numbers to before, which is why no existing golden case moved.

Editing is inline on the account card (`AccountsStep.tsx`), never a separate
step: a deposit always belongs to one account. Below `lg`, account and deposit
edit fields and closed deposit rows stack one per row (desktop stays a
wrapping row). See first-plan-onboarding for the wizard-wide mobile stack. Deposits are hard-deleted, with
no soft-delete trash of their own. Enter in a deposit field is Done; it must
stop bubbling so the parent account card stays open. The projection table
shows a "Deposits into accounts" section and the cash-flow chart a deposits
series, both hidden when every year is zero.

## Gotchas

- `migrateHousehold` defaults `deposits` to `[]`, drops entries with a
  non-finite `amount` or `startYear` (the engine assumes a placeable deposit),
  and strips the legacy per-deposit `growthRate`.
- A one-time deposit ignores `endYear` and only ever pays in `startYear`.
- Open-ended deposits (no `endYear`) are bounded by the caller's `throughYear`
  in `deposits.ts`, so nothing loops forever.
- New deposits default `endYear` to the account owner's retirement year. On a
  joint after-tax account that is the later of the two people (see
  `accountDepositEndYear`). Existing deposits keep the year the user set.
